600 Series Dealer Programmer 
-----------------------------

The Dealer Programmer will allow you to configure the
features of the 600 series radios.

Windows and DOS versions of the Dealer Programmer are
available.

The Windows version can be invoked by 'double clicking'
on the wdps_dp icon (from the group folder) or by double
clicking on the 'wdps_dp.exe' file from the windows file
manager.

The DOS version can be invoked by entering 'ddps_dp' on
the DOS command line (having firstly changed directory to
that where the programmer is installed).

Details of how to use the programmer can be found in
the user manual and help text, both of which can be
accessed from within the programmer (from the HELP
menu).

Recent enhancements
-------------------

The following enhancements have been made to the
programmer since version R00.02.21 :-

. It is now possible to save both network and personality
  data in one file. The file type for the combined data
  is 'Radio' and has default file extension of '.rad'.
  The Format field on the File Save window must be set to
  'Radio' when saving such a file.

. Entry to the service windows is now password protected
  (to discourage inadvertant modification of tuning data).

. It is now possible to configure the programmer to search a
  specified directory for data files (as opposed to looking
  in the directory in which the DPS is installed). The
  directory can be specified at installation and can be
  modified from the programmer's Options/Configure menu.

. The programmer supports a number of additional radio
  models e.g. for VHF radios.

. A second service window has been provided for tuning Bias
  Voltage. The second window is only applicable to certain
  radio models and will only be accessible when attempting
  to tune such models (it will be 'greyed out' for models
  that only have one bias voltage level).
  Note that Bias Voltage tuning should only be necessary if
  the radio's power transistors have been replaced.

. Additional changes have been made to increase usability.
  These include setting the main window to maximum size on
  entry to the programmer, improvements to help text for
  service windows and rearrangement of MPT personality
  window.

The following additional enhancements have been made to the
programmer since version R00.03.02 :-

. It is now possible to program data for one radio model
  into a different radio model, provided that the data
  to be programmed is compatible with the new model.

. Once data has been read into the programmer it will be
  possible to change the model number associated with that
  data. The function to change model is accessible from the
  programmer's EDIT menu.

. After a file has been saved, the status bar will be
  updated with the name of the saved file.

. The programmer supports a number of additional radio
  models e.g. for 300 MHz radios.


Preferential NDD problem resolved
---------------------------------

The DPS has been modified to rectify a problem concerning
the priority of Preferential NDD subset hunt.

With the previous DPS, the priority of Preferential NDD
subset hunt was reversed i.e. highest priority = 10,
lowest priority = 1.

This problem has now been corrected such that highest
priority = 1 and lowest priority = 10.

Please note that radios programmed with previous versions
of the DPS will result in Preferential NDD subset hunt of
reversed priority as compared to that which was programmed.
On reading such radios the new DPS will show the actual
Preferential NDD priority (highest = 1, lowest = 10), which
may then be corrected as outlined below :-

 1. Read radio using the Dealer Programmer.
 2. Save Network data to disk.
 3. Exit from the Dealer Programmer.
 4. Invoke Network Programmer and read the saved network
    data file.
 5. Open the 'Preferential NDD Data' window from the 'Edit'
    menu of the Network Programmer and modify the priority
    setting as required.
 6. Save the modified network data to disk.
 7. Exit from the Network Programmer.
 8. Read the radio using the Dealer Programmer and then
    read the modified network data file.
 9. Program the radio.
    
